UPDATE lessons SET title='Year 4, Week 12, Lesson 2: Mapping the region', data=json_patch(data,'{"child_mission":"Mapping the Asia–Pacific region uses scale, keys and consistent symbols so distances and locations can be compared fairly.","coming_soon":null,"duration_minutes":55,"evidence_instructions":"Save the independent response, specific revisions and both short-answer explanations; record oral evidence for speaking tasks.","explicit_teaching":null,"extension":"Apply the same HSIE idea to a new place or source, then compare two pieces of evidence for strength.","follow_up_challenges":null,"guided_practice":"PRACTISE BEFORE READING THE FEEDBACK\nWhy must you use the map’s scale instead of guessing centimetres as kilometres?\n\nFEEDBACK\nScale converts map distance to real distance; guessing invents false distances.\n\nWATCH FOR\nA map without a key or scale cannot support accurate comparison.","hoard_words":null,"independent_task":"INDEPENDENT EVIDENCE\nOn a regional map, answer eight questions using key and scale. Record two measured distances and one limitation of a flat map.\n\nWrite a short reflection naming one source or idea you corrected and why.","interactive_activities":null,"learning_area":"HSIE","learning_intention":"Mapping the Asia–Pacific region uses scale, keys and consistent symbols so distances and locations can be compared fairly.","lesson_number":2,"lesson_role":"Apply and respond","materials":["Paper or typed answer space","Pencil","Optional: local map, photo or interview notes"],"offline_alternative":"Use paper and pencil to complete the original task, then retain a photograph or short parent observation as evidence.","outcome_codes":["HS2-GEO-01"],"parent_notes":"Parent: Marking guidance for the independent task: On a regional map, answer eight questions using key and scale. Record two measured distances and one limitation of a flat map. Accept equivalent original responses that show the same skill.\n\nParent: Written model 1: Original response on new material: Scale converts map distance to real distance; guessing invents false distances. Reasoning: I used map by attending to region rather than copying the worked model.\n\nParent: Written model 2: Common error: A map without a key or scale cannot support accurate comparison. Correction: Scale converts map distance to real distance; guessing invents false distances. Why: the correction restores accurate use of map.\n\nParent: Likely misconception: A map without a key or scale cannot support accurate comparison. Keep sample answers out of the child role.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 1: Produce a fresh example demonstrating map on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 2: Name one likely error with map, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.\n\nParent: Marking notes: Require independently produced work with a specific supporting detail. / Require misconception + correction + purpose-linked reason..","pass":1,"pass_mark":0.9,"planner_fields":null,"planner_intro":null,"planner_title":null,"printable_resource":null,"quiz":[{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Scale converts map distance to real distance; guessing invents false distances. This matches the lesson skill for map.","options":["A map without a key or scale cannot support accurate comparison.","Ignore the source and invent a more exciting story.","Copy the model places and call that independent work.","Scale converts map distance to real distance; guessing invents false distances."],"question":"Why must you use the map’s scale instead of guessing centimetres as kilometres?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"A map without a key or scale cannot support accurate comparison. That misconception weakens accuracy; the other choices align with the lesson idea.","options":["Mapping the Asia–Pacific region uses scale, keys and consistent symbols so distances and locations can be compared fairly.","Scale converts map distance to real distance; guessing invents false distances.","Careful use of region with clear evidence.","A map without a key or scale cannot support accurate comparison."],"question":"Which statement is a common mistake when practising map?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Understanding is shown by new evidence and named region, not by copying the model.","options":["Treat a missing fact as if it were recorded in a source.","Apply the idea with new evidence and name the region used.","Repeat the worked example wording with no new evidence.","Choose randomly because any option could be right."],"question":"A learner is practising map. Which approach best shows understanding?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Scale converts map distance to real distance; guessing invents false distances. uses sources and places appropriately for this lesson.","options":["Copy the model places and call that independent work.","Scale converts map distance to real distance; guessing invents false distances.","A map without a key or scale cannot support accurate comparison.","Ignore the source and invent a more exciting story."],"question":"Which response best shows careful thinking about map?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Strong answers name specific evidence and what it shows.","options":["Name a specific source, cue or example and say what it shows about map.","Make a bigger claim without naming any source or cue.","Copy a classmate’s sentence with no local detail.","A map without a key or scale cannot support accurate comparison."],"question":"Which habit strengthens evidence about region?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"The stronger choice matches the taught idea with concrete detail.","options":["Treat a missing fact as if it were recorded in a source.","Ignore the source and invent a more exciting story.","Example: a regional map with a scale bar; students measure approximate distance between two capitals and compare with a globe check.","A map without a key or scale cannot support accurate comparison."],"question":"Using this lesson idea — Mapping the Asia–Pacific region uses scale, keys and consistent symbols so distances and locations can be… — which choice is best?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Respect and accuracy mean checking evidence and honouring limits/protocols.","options":["Check facts/cues, acknowledge limits, and avoid inventing details.","Invent missing details to make the answer sound complete.","Ignore safety or cultural protocols to finish faster.","Treat every online claim as equally reliable."],"question":"Which action shows respect and accuracy together?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Independence requires a new example that still uses the skill.","options":["Copy the model places and call that independent work.","A map without a key or scale cannot support accurate comparison.","On a regional map, answer eight questions using key and scale. Record two measured distances and one limitation of a flat map.","Retype the worked example with no new example."],"question":"Which task best shows independent use of map?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"explanation":"Independent evidence on new material demonstrates the skill; copying the model does not.","marking_guide":"Require independently produced work with a specific supporting detail.","question":"Produce a fresh example demonstrating map on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.","sample_answer":"Original response on new material: Scale converts map distance to real distance; guessing invents false distances. Reasoning: I used map by attending to region rather than copying the worked model.","type":"short_answer"},{"explanation":"A useful correction changes the inaccurate idea or method and explains why.","marking_guide":"Require misconception + correction + purpose-linked reason.","question":"Name one likely error with map, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.","sample_answer":"Common error: A map without a key or scale cannot support accurate comparison. Correction: Scale converts map distance to real distance; guessing invents false distances. Why: the correction restores accurate use of map.","type":"short_answer"}],"resources":null,"response_prompt":"Include your independent evidence and reflection, with reasons where requested.","review_status":"unreviewed","seed_key":"s2-hsie-y4-w12-l2","sort_activity":null,"source_note":"Proposed Stage 2 NESA HSIE alignment using catalogue outcome codes (HS2-*); content-point mapping and local First Nations protocols remain pending human review.","spelling":null,"spelling_focus":null,"stage":"S2","stage_hints":{"accept":["Today you will practise map.","Read the mission before you start mapping or using sources.","Keep the place, people or source you are studying in view."],"guided":["Attempt the guided prompt without looking ahead.","Afterwards, compare your evidence with the feedback.","Adjust anything that does not match the method."],"learn":["Study how the first demonstration is built.","Point to the source or place detail that carries the idea.","Then write the reason without copying every sentence."],"quiz":["Read every option before you choose.","Eliminate answers that ignore sources or respectful practice.","Prefer accurate evidence over a guess that merely looks neat."],"submit":["Confirm that your evidence and reason are both present.","Proofread names, places, dates and sequence.","Make sure the final evidence matches the lesson purpose."],"teach0":["Read the stage title and find the worked model.","Name one exact source detail or place feature before you decide.","Try the small prompt before opening the comparison."],"teach1":["Return to the model and list the key evidence.","Say what each source or place detail shows about the idea.","A final label alone is weaker than recorded evidence."],"teach2":["Compare a weak claim with stronger evidence.","Change the claim or use of sources, not only how neat the poster looks.","Name the mistake you are repairing."],"teach3":["Choose a fresh place, person or source that still uses the same skill.","Write one reason in your own words.","Do not retype the worked model as your answer."],"write":["Follow every part of the independent instructions.","Include original evidence and a short reason.","Save work that shows the skill on new sources or places."]},"subject":"Geography","success_criteria":["I can use map with a specific example from this lesson.","I can identify region and explain why it matters.","I can practise scale independently on a new prompt.","I can notice key needs and correct a likely mistake."],"suspense_builder":null,"teach_steps":[{"check":{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Mapping the Asia–Pacific region uses scale, keys and consistent symbols so distances and locations can be compared fairly. Supported records follow the actual sources and purpose of the task.","options":["Mapping the Asia–Pacific region uses scale, keys and consistent symbols so distances and locations can be compared fairly.","A claim unrelated to the source or place.","An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no evidence."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about map?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nExample: a regional map with a scale bar; students measure approximate distance between two capitals and compare with a globe check.\n\nTRY FIRST\nWhy must you use the map’s scale instead of guessing centimetres as kilometres?\n\nFEEDBACK\nScale converts map distance to real distance; guessing invents false distances.","explain":"Mapping the Asia–Pacific region uses scale, keys and consistent symbols so distances and locations can be compared fairly.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner base claims on sources, places and respectful community knowledge, rather than guessing from a single picture. Keep every claim linked to evidence.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the sources, map features or community examples that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Compare your record with the feedback: did you use the same skill and show your evidence? If a detail is uncertain, identify what source you would need next. One deliberate, evidence-based revision is more useful than writing a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"A map without a key or scale cannot support accurate comparison. Correct the claim or use of sources, not just the neatness of the poster.","title":"Understand and model","visual":[{"alt":"Mapping the region.","caption":"Use scale, do not guess.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Scale bar</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">real distance</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Key</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">symbols</text></g></svg>"}],"visual_before":[{"episode":{"scenes":[{"bg":"room","lines":[{"text":"I can see the example, but I am not sure which source or place detail matters most.","who":"pip"},{"text":"Name the sources, map features or community examples that carry the idea before you decide.","who":"juno"},{"text":"I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.","who":"pip"},{"pause_after":true,"text":"Pause here. Use this model: Example: a regional map with a scale bar; students measure approximate distance between two capitals and compare with a  Then write your own evidence.","who":"juno"}]}],"title":"Talk through the first model","transcript":"Pip: I can see the example, but I am not sure which source or place detail matters most.\nJuno: Name the sources, map features or community examples that carry the idea before you decide.\nPip: I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.\nJuno: Pause here. Use this model: Example: a regional map with a scale bar; students measure approximate distance between two capitals and compare with a  Then write your own evidence."}}]},{"check":{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Make the HSIE idea visible: name the observation, source or example you used and say why it supports the claim. Supported records follow the actual sources and purpose of the task.","options":["An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no evidence.","Make the HSIE idea visible: name the observation, source or example you used and say why it supports the claim.","A claim unrelated to the source or place."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about map?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nExample: a regional map with a scale bar; students measure approximate distance between two capitals and compare with a globe check. Look at each observation, source detail or example that carries the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nList the key observations, sources or steps from the model and say what each one shows.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe most useful record names the evidence and the reason it supports the claim, not only a label.","explain":"Make the HSIE idea visible: name the observation, source or example you used and say why it supports the claim.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner base claims on sources, places and respectful community knowledge, rather than guessing from a single picture. Keep every claim linked to evidence.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the sources, map features or community examples that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Compare your record with the feedback: did you use the same skill and show your evidence? If a detail is uncertain, identify what source you would need next. One deliberate, evidence-based revision is more useful than writing a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Writing only a final label without evidence does not show HSIE thinking. Correct the claim or use of sources, not just the neatness of the poster.","title":"Show the evidence","visual":[{"alt":"Scale practice.","caption":"Flat-map limit.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Eight questions</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">two distances</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Risk</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">cm = km myth</text></g></svg>"}]},{"check":{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Use an intentionally incorrect claim as a comparison. Name its specific HSIE error, then show the corrected evidence-based claim. Supported records follow the actual sources and purpose of the task.","options":["Use an intentionally incorrect claim as a comparison. Name its specific HSIE error, then show the corrected evidence-based claim.","A claim unrelated to the source or place.","An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no evidence."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about map?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nA map without a key or scale cannot support accurate comparison. Here is a more productive model: Example: a regional map with a scale bar; students measure approximate distance between two capitals and compare with a globe check.\n\nTRY FIRST\nDescribe why the warned-against claim fails, then write a corrected claim with evidence